WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.878
When a Union law and a State

2
00:00:01.878 --> 00:00:03.855
law clash, the Union usually wins.

3
00:00:04.105 --> 00:00:05.108
Usually.

4
00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:07.840
Article 246 splits law-making

5
00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.149
into three lists:

6
00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:11.104
Union, State, and Concurrent.

7
00:00:11.504 --> 00:00:13.599
A subject on no list belongs

8
00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:16.069
to Parliament, under Article 248.

9
00:00:16.519 --> 00:00:18.052
If a State law clashes with

10
00:00:18.052 --> 00:00:20.381
Parliament's law on a Concurrent subject,

11
00:00:20.481 --> 00:00:21.974
the State law is void to

12
00:00:21.974 --> 00:00:22.721
that extent.

13
00:00:23.121 --> 00:00:24.998
Unless the President assents to it.

14
00:00:25.198 --> 00:00:26.798
Then it prevails in that State,

15
00:00:26.898 --> 00:00:28.903
until Parliament legislates again.

16
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:31.956
And Article 356, President's Rule?

17
00:00:32.156 --> 00:00:34.074
In Bommai, in 1994, nine

18
00:00:34.074 --> 00:00:37.191
judges made it open to judicial review,

19
00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:39.296
with majority tested on the floor.

20
00:00:39.746 --> 00:00:41.282
The lists divide power.

21
00:00:41.482 --> 00:00:43.146
The courts guard the balance.

22
00:00:43.646 --> 00:00:44.542
Your turn.

23
00:00:44.842 --> 00:00:46.684
Who holds powers over subjects

24
00:00:46.684 --> 00:00:47.359
on no list?
